  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Short-term memory: the thread",
      "items": [
        "The thread's messages live in the checkpointer, one state per thread_id",
        "After a reply, once the thread passes CONTEXT_MAX_TOKENS (10,000), older turns are folded into one running summary; about 5,000 tokens of recent turns stay, cut at a turn boundary",
        "The turns folded away become a summary push into long-term memory",
        "Past 6,000 tokens the model is sent only the latest 3 tool results; the thread keeps all of them"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Long-term memory: the user",
      "items": [
        "Small memories, one fact each, at (\"memories\", user_id), with a vector index on their content",
        "Every turn LongTermMemory searches them with the latest user message (MEMORY_RECALL_LIMIT 10) and adds the ones the thread hasn't recalled yet to its state; the system prompt lists them",
        "A write or delete bumps the user's memory version, so threads recall afresh",
        "Temporary facts carry an end date: the store's TTL hides them, the sweeper deletes them hourly",
        "If embeddings are down, the turn goes on with what the thread already recalled"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "What a push does",
      "items": [
        "The memory model lists the facts the user stated, each with a scope, a topic and an end date if temporary",
        "Facts with redaction placeholders or claims of status, discounts or permissions are dropped in code",
        "For each fact, the 5 closest existing memories are found, and the model decides: add it, update the ones it corrects, delete the ones it makes obsolete",
        "One push per user at a time (a Postgres advisory lock); a per-thread cursor means no turn is pushed twice"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "What gets enqueued",
      "items": [
        "summary: the thread passed CONTEXT_MAX_TOKENS; the job carries the turns being folded away",
        "remember: the Remember this button (question + reply) or the remember tool (the user's words)",
        "idle: the thread has been idle 30 min and changed since its last push; the job holds only ids, and the worker reads the thread from the checkpointer",
        "No SQS_QUEUE_URL: no queue at all, every push runs in the process that triggered it"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Order, retries, dead letters",
      "items": [
        "MessageGroupId is the user: one user's pushes run in order, one at a time; different users run side by side",
        "The dedupe id makes the same push enqueued twice within 5 min run once",
        "The worker deletes a message only after its push succeeds; otherwise it comes back after the 300 s visibility timeout",
        "After 5 receives the queue moves it to the DLQ, and ChatbotMemoryPushesDeadLettered fires"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "When SendMessage fails",
      "items": [
        "Retried after 0.2 s and 0.5 s; after the third failure the push runs in the pod that triggered it",
        "QUEUE_FALLBACKS counts each one, so a push is never dropped while the queue is down",
        "The idle scan uses the same path, so in the worker the fallback runs in the worker"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "The worker is always on",
      "items": [
        "A Deployment with 1 replica and the Recreate strategy: never two idle scanners, even mid-rollout",
        "Four things in one process: the consumer loop, the idle scan, the store's TTL sweeper and metrics on :9100 (pushes, queue depth, job age)",
        "The API pods set BACKGROUND_JOBS=false; under make dev the API runs the idle scan itself"
      ]
    }
  ]
